1
00:00:00,125 --> 00:00:01,300
How many pieces?

2
00:00:01,480 --> 00:00:03,820
Bend a spaghetti stick
until it snaps.

3
00:00:04,153 --> 00:00:06,993
Hold both ends and push
them together, slowly.

4
00:00:07,358 --> 00:00:09,538
The stick curves,
then snaps.

5
00:00:09,698 --> 00:00:11,278
You expect two halves.

6
00:00:11,423 --> 00:00:13,543
You almost always
get three or more.

7
00:00:13,987 --> 00:00:17,222
Slowed down, the first
break isn't the end.

8
00:00:17,242 --> 00:00:19,172
A moment later,
it snaps again,

9
00:00:19,172 --> 00:00:20,127
and again.

10
00:00:20,487 --> 00:00:23,067
The physicist Richard
Feynman noticed this,

11
00:00:23,067 --> 00:00:24,752
and asked why.

12
00:00:24,792 --> 00:00:26,532
Years later,
two physicists

13
00:00:26,532 --> 00:00:27,867
explained it.

14
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:30,807
The first break sends
ripples along the stick.

15
00:00:30,807 --> 00:00:32,917
They bend it even more
than before,

16
00:00:32,917 --> 00:00:35,237
and that extra bend
breaks it again.

17
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:41,060
In 2018, a team
at MIT found a trick.

18
00:00:41,215 --> 00:00:44,345
Twist the stick first,
over two thirds of a turn.

19
00:00:44,555 --> 00:00:47,365
Then bend it:
usually just two pieces.

20
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:53,875
Measure it: little twist,
three or more pieces.

21
00:00:54,305 --> 00:00:57,675
Past about 250 degrees,
two pieces win.

22
00:00:58,153 --> 00:01:00,068
It depends
on how you break it.

23
00:01:00,068 --> 00:01:02,588
Bend slowly:
a chain of snaps.

24
00:01:02,913 --> 00:01:05,698
Twist first:
usually one clean break.

25
00:01:06,187 --> 00:01:08,242
Next: a jar of nuts.

26
00:01:08,407 --> 00:01:10,200
The biggest one tends
to climb.
